{
  "requirement": "Each public SDK item has one defining module path. The SDK exposes no public\nre-exports. The chapter that introduces each supported type or free function owns its declaration.\nThe [public API inventory](sdk/public-api.md) links each item to that requirement,\nwhich describes its purpose\nand declares its public Rust interface, including fields, enum variants, trait\nmembers, and inherent methods. A change to that interface requires a change to\nthe owning requirement. Every supported struct with public fields is\n`#[non_exhaustive]`. Its constructor accepts the values needed to create it.\nAdditional fields have consuming builder methods that accept a concrete value.\nOptional fields start as `None`. Backend inference options start empty and can\nbe added one key at a time.\n\nThe SDK’s public modules follow the interfaces introduced in these chapters.\n\n- `domain`: application data and the `Domain`, `NodeContents`, and `SourceNodes` traits.\n- `graph`: criterion definitions, graph observations, and evidence; `graph::validator`\n  runs criteria.\n- `request`: `ValidationContext`, typed `Ask` builders, context values, and request phases.\n- `agent`: configuration for the supplied agents.\n- `sources`: project inspection, search queries, and their results.\n- `outline`: selectable source contents and language rules; `outline::languages`\n  supplies bundled languages, and `outline::selector` defines selector syntax.\n- `storage`: filesystem evidence storage.\n\nSource access, search engines, redaction, provider transports, agent-result paging,\nand conversation machinery are implementation details of those interfaces.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.sdk.public_api`. Each obligation maps to at least one test with assertions that would fail if the obligation were broken.\n\n**One defining path and no public re-exports**\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[sdk_has_no_public_reexports]` parses every `.rs` file under `src` with tree-sitter and fails if any `use_declaration` carries a plain `pub` visibility modifier. That covers \"no public re-exports\".\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` runs `cargo doc` on the current tree. `documented_exports` walks the rustdoc item tables and rejects unknown categories, duplicate exports, and any item missing from the inventory. The test then asserts that the inventory (`specified_exports`) and the rustdoc exports have no missing or unexpected entries, by path and kind. That covers one defining module path and the module and item list.\n\n**Inventory links each item to its owning requirement, and the interface matches it**\n- `specified_exports` enforces the shape of each inventory entry. Every non-module item needs a declaration link, the link must resolve to a `habeas-req-requirements.sdk.<chapter>.` anchor, and members must be nested and unique.\n- `check_declaration` finds the owning requirement in the chapter. It requires a prose contract and a Rust declaration. It compares trait impls and member names. It compares `signature_parts` of the requirement's declaration against the rustdoc declaration, covering fields, enum variants, trait members and inherent methods. This enforces \"a change to the interface requires a change to the owning requirement\".\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed]` checks that the comparison helper behaves correctly. Reordering members is accepted. Changing a field type, a return type or `&mut self` is rejected, and `(T,)` is kept distinct from `(T)`.\n\n**Every public-field struct is `#[non_exhaustive]`**\n- `check_declaration` asserts `#[non_exhaustive]` on any published struct whose page has `structfield.` members. This covers every declared struct automatically.\n\n**Constructors, consuming builders, `None` defaults, and backend options**\n- `src/agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` covers the backend config structs:\n  - Constructors take the required values. `BedrockConfig::new` and `OllamaConfig::new` take a model, and `OpenAiConfig::new` takes nothing.\n  - Optional fields start as `None`, shown by serialization that omits them.\n  - Options start empty and are added one key at a time. Setting key `a` twice leaves one `a` with the later value, and key `b` is kept.\n  - `BedrockCredentials::new` and the consuming `session_token` builder are exercised.\n- `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` checks that `name_span` starts as `None` and attachments start empty. It also checks that the consuming builders set them, and that `with_attachments` replaces rather than appends.\n- `tests/source_defaults.rs` covers the source types:\n  - `fn[source_bytes_error_kinds_and_language_names_round_trip]` checks that `SourceFile::new` starts with `language` as `None` and that the `language` builder sets it.\n  - `fn[scoped_text_results_keep_line_numbers_context_and_completeness]` checks `TextQuery` defaults (`limit` is `None`, `context_lines` is 0) and the scope, `context_lines` and `limit` builders.\n  - `fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` checks `StructuralQuery::limit` and `scope`.\n- `src/outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]` covers `Segment::new`, the consuming `ordinal` builder, and `SelectorError::new`.\n\n**Not separately tested**\n- Constructors that only store their arguments, such as `Fingerprint::new`, `Snapshot::new`, `Span::new`, `TextMatch::new` and `SearchResults::new`, have no conditional or transformation. Their signatures are still checked against the owning requirement through `check_declaration`, so they need no separate test.\n- Behavior of the individual owning requirements is delegated to their own tests.\n\nI found no unaddressed obligation, and no {fixme} acknowledgments apply.",
